================================================================================
Origin and acknowledgement
================================================================================

andes has its own peptide-spectrum scoring, its own statistical models trained
on public PRIDE data, and an independent Rust implementation.

andes began as a fork of MS-GF+ (Kim & Pevzner, Nat Commun 2014;5:5277) and was
independently rewritten in Rust. Its generating-function scoring was replaced
with an empirical rank/intensity model, and its bundled scoring models are
trained on public data — so the current engine and models share no code or model
data with MS-GF+, which remains the cited origin and inspiration.

Its design also draws on ideas from the broader open-source proteomics
search-engine community, and we gratefully acknowledge:

  - X!Tandem  (Craig & Beavis, Bioinformatics 2004)
  - Comet     (Eng, Jahan & Hoopmann, Proteomics 2013)
  - Sage      (Lazear, J. Proteome Res. 2023)
